from qb import Q, O

Q("P2-Q01", "1.5",
  "Policy says the support agent may issue refunds up to $500 on its own; anything larger needs a human decision. Despite a clear system prompt rule, about 3% of refund calls above $500 are still executed by the agent. The team wants the policy to hold every time while keeping customers moving. What should they implement?",
  [O("A pre-call hook on process_refund that blocks amounts above $500 and redirects the case to the escalate_to_human workflow with the case details.", True,
     "Correct. The hook enforces the threshold deterministically and keeps the customer moving by redirecting to the right workflow."),
   O("A stronger system prompt that repeats the $500 limit in capital letters at the start and end of the instructions and adds two examples of correct behavior.", False,
     "Emphasis and examples reduce but do not eliminate violations. A prompt-based rule keeps a non-zero failure rate, which is unacceptable for a financial limit."),
   O("A nightly report listing refunds above $500 so a manager can reverse any that should not have been issued autonomously.", False,
     "Detection after the fact leaves the money already paid out and relies on a reversal that may not be possible or timely."),
   O("A requirement that the agent self-report its confidence before each refund and skip any refund where confidence is below 90 percent.", False,
     "Confidence does not track the dollar amount, and self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated. The rule is about amount, not certainty.")],
  "Hooks that intercept outgoing tool calls enforce compliance rules deterministically, for example blocking refunds above a threshold and redirecting to an alternative workflow such as human escalation. The business rule is an exact numeric limit, so code should apply it rather than the model.",
  "Tool call interception hooks versus prompt-based enforcement (Task 1.5, 1.4).",
  "A residual 3% violation rate under a clear prompt shows the instruction is being followed probabilistically. A hard financial threshold needs a guarantee.",
  "When a rule can be expressed as a check on tool arguments, enforce it in code and give the agent a safe alternative path.")

Q("P2-Q02", "1.1",
  "In testing, Claude sometimes responds with two tool_use blocks in one turn: get_customer and lookup_order, which are independent. The developer's loop executes only the first block and sends back one tool_result. The next API call fails or the agent behaves strangely. What is the correct handling?",
  [O("Execute every tool_use block in the response and return a matching tool_result for each tool_use ID in the next message, then continue the loop.", True,
     "Correct. Each tool request needs its own result so the model can reason about all of them."),
   O("Execute only the first tool_use block and discard the second, since the model will request it again if it is still needed.", False,
     "The unanswered tool_use block leaves the history inconsistent, and the model does not simply re-ask."),
   O("Treat a response with more than one tool_use block as an error and ask the model to retry with a single tool call.", False,
     "Parallel tool requests are valid. Rejecting them wastes a round trip and discards a useful capability."),
   O("Combine both results into a single tool_result block tied to the first tool_use ID and ignore the second ID.", False,
     "Every tool_use ID needs its own matching tool_result, so merging them leaves the second request unanswered.")],
  "When stop_reason is 'tool_use', the response may contain several tool_use blocks. The loop must run all of them and append tool_result blocks that correspond to each tool_use ID so the model sees the outcome of every request before deciding the next step.",
  "Agentic loop mechanics and tool results in history (Task 1.1).",
  "Two independent tool requests in one response and a loop that handles only one explains the odd behavior.",
  "Treat tool calls as a list, not a single value. Pair each request with its result.", diff="C")

Q("P2-Q03", "1.5",
  "Order lookups sometimes return payment details including full card numbers, and the model then repeats them in its replies. Compliance wants the model never to see full card numbers, while it still needs the last four digits to confirm which card was charged. Which design best meets this?",
  [O("A PostToolUse hook that masks card numbers down to the last four digits in tool results before the model processes them.", True,
     "Correct. The model receives only the masked value, so it cannot repeat what it never saw."),
   O("A system prompt instruction telling the agent never to write a full card number in any reply and to mask digits when it does mention a card.", False,
     "The model still receives the sensitive value and can leak it when the prompt rule fails."),
   O("A post-processing filter on the final customer message that removes any 16-digit sequence before the reply is sent.", False,
     "This helps at the output boundary but the sensitive data is still in the model's context, logs, and any subagent handoffs."),
   O("Remove lookup_order from the agent's tools and have staff paste payment details into the chat when needed.", False,
     "This breaks the workflow and moves sensitive data handling to humans without need.")],
  "PostToolUse hooks intercept tool results before the model processes them. Masking sensitive fields there applies least privilege to data: the model gets what it needs (last four digits) and nothing more.",
  "PostToolUse hooks for transformation and data minimization (Task 1.5).",
  "The requirement that the model never see full numbers rules out approaches that act only after the model has the data.",
  "Minimize what enters the context. Data the model never sees cannot be leaked by it.", diff="F")

Q("P2-Q04", "1.6",
  "A support organization runs a weekly quality audit of closed tickets. Every ticket must be checked for the same four aspects: policy compliance, tone, correct tool use, and accuracy of the resolution. Reviewers want consistent, repeatable results. Which decomposition fits best?",
  [O("A fixed sequential prompt chain with one focused step per aspect, each step receiving the ticket and the previous findings as needed.", True,
     "Correct. The aspects are known in advance, so a predictable chain gives consistent, auditable results."),
   O("A dynamic plan where the model decides at runtime which aspects to check for each ticket and in what order.", False,
     "Adaptive planning suits open-ended investigation. Here it would make the audit inconsistent between tickets."),
   O("A single request asking for a complete audit of all four aspects in one response, with a list of criteria at the end.", False,
     "One broad pass tends to give uneven depth across aspects."),
   O("Four independent agents with their own tools that never share findings and a coordinator that picks the one with the longest report.", False,
     "Independent agents add coordination cost, and selecting by report length is not a quality criterion.")],
  "Prompt chaining suits predictable multi-aspect reviews where the steps are known ahead of time. Dynamic decomposition suits open-ended tasks where subtasks depend on what is discovered.",
  "Choosing between prompt chaining and dynamic decomposition (Task 1.6).",
  "The same four aspects for every ticket mean the work is predictable, which favors a fixed chain.",
  "Use the least dynamic structure that fits the task.", diff="F")

Q("P2-Q05", "1.4",
  "The agent calls process_refund and the gateway responds slowly. The agent's tool wrapper times out, retries the call, and the customer is refunded twice. Logs show the first call actually succeeded. What is the most reliable fix?",
  [O("Have the tool layer attach an idempotency key derived from the order and request, so repeated calls for the same refund return the original result instead of paying again.", True,
     "Correct. Idempotency makes a retry safe regardless of whether the first attempt succeeded."),
   O("Add a system prompt rule telling the agent to call lookup_order before every retry to check whether the refund was already issued.", False,
     "The agent may skip the check, and there is still a race between the check and the retry."),
   O("Remove the retry logic so that any timeout is reported to the customer as a failure and left for a human to resolve.", False,
     "This avoids duplicates but turns every slow response into an unresolved case, hurting first-contact resolution."),
   O("Lower the timeout so the wrapper gives up faster and reports failures sooner.", False,
     "A shorter timeout increases the number of ambiguous outcomes. It does not make retries safe.")],
  "A timeout does not tell you whether the side effect happened. Making the operation idempotent, with a stable key per intended refund, lets the system retry safely. Guarantees for financial actions belong in the tool and its wrapper, not in model discipline.",
  "Deterministic safeguards against duplicate side effects (Task 1.4, 1.5).",
  "The first call succeeded despite the timeout, which is the classic ambiguous-outcome case that idempotency addresses.",
  "Every retried write needs an idempotency story.", diff="F")

Q("P2-Q06", "1.6",
  "A customer asks about three separate orders in one message: delivery status for order A, a return on order B, and a billing question on order C. The lookup for order C times out while A and B succeed. Which behavior is best?",
  [O("Handle the concerns as separate items, answer A and B with their results, and clearly tell the customer that C could not be checked yet, offering a retry or escalation.", True,
     "Correct. Partial results are delivered and the failure is stated rather than hidden."),
   O("Hold the entire reply until C succeeds, retrying silently, so the customer receives one complete answer.", False,
     "Silent retries can stall the whole response and the customer receives nothing meanwhile."),
   O("Reply only about the order that succeeded first and ignore the other two to keep the answer short.", False,
     "Ignoring concerns leaves the request unresolved."),
   O("Report that all three lookups failed so the answer is consistent, and ask the customer to try again later.", False,
     "That misstates what happened and discards the valid results.")],
  "Multi-concern requests should be decomposed, with each concern investigated and the results synthesized. When one lookup fails, local recovery comes first, and anything unresolved is reported honestly alongside the valid results rather than hidden or allowed to block everything.",
  "Decomposition with partial failure handling (Task 1.6, 2.2, 5.3).",
  "Two of three succeeded, so a good design preserves their value and is transparent about the third.",
  "Return what you know, say what you don't, and offer a next step.", diff="C")

Q("P2-Q07", "1.5",
  "A team is deciding which of its support agent requirements to implement as hooks versus prompt instructions. Which requirement is best enforced with a hook?",
  [O("Refunds over $500 must be routed to a human instead of being issued by the agent.", True,
     "Correct. It is a hard, checkable rule with financial consequences, which needs deterministic enforcement."),
   O("Replies should sound warm and empathetic while staying concise.", False,
     "Tone is a judgment-based style preference, which belongs in the prompt. A hook cannot meaningfully check it."),
   O("When an order number is mentioned, the agent should generally prefer lookup_order over get_customer.", False,
     "This is tool-selection guidance, better handled by tool descriptions and examples."),
   O("The agent should summarize what it did at the end of each conversation.", False,
     "A closing summary is a soft behavior expectation. A missed summary has low cost.")],
  "Hooks provide deterministic guarantees where business rules require compliance. Prompt instructions are appropriate for soft guidance such as tone and tool preferences, where occasional deviation is tolerable.",
  "Choosing hooks versus prompts (Task 1.5).",
  "Only the refund threshold is both objectively checkable and costly if violated.",
  "Use code for rules and prompts for judgment.", diff="I")

Q("P2-Q08", "2.2",
  "A customer asks, 'Where are my recent orders?' The order service is down, and the MCP tool returns an empty list marked as success. The agent tells the customer they have no orders. What is the best tool design?",
  [O("Return an error result with category transient, isRetryable true, and a description, reserving an empty successful result for a lookup that ran and found nothing.", True,
     "Correct. The agent can tell an outage from a real empty history and can retry or apologize accordingly."),
   O("Keep returning an empty list in both cases, but add a free-text note field that is sometimes filled in on errors.", False,
     "Overloading the empty success hides the distinction unless the model reliably reads an optional note."),
   O("Return the HTTP status code number as the tool output and let the model interpret it.", False,
     "Raw status codes are not a stable contract and do not convey retryability or a customer-friendly description."),
   O("Have the agent call the tool three times and treat three empty results as proof the customer has no orders.", False,
     "Repeated calls to a down service return the same misleading result.")],
  "Access failures need retry decisions, while valid empty results are successful queries with no matches. Returning structured errors with category and retryability prevents the agent from converting an outage into a false statement.",
  "Access failures versus valid empty results (Task 2.2).",
  "The incorrect statement 'you have no orders' is what happens when failure is reported as emptiness.",
  "Make failure impossible to confuse with absence.", diff="C")

Q("P2-Q09", "2.4",
  "Your team wants the agent to read and update tickets in a standard ticketing product, and also to issue refunds under your company-specific approval rules. Developers propose building both integrations as custom MCP servers. What is the best approach?",
  [O("Use an existing community MCP server for the standard ticketing integration and build a custom server only for the refund logic, which is specific to your workflows.", True,
     "Correct. Reuse for standard integrations, custom only where the logic is yours."),
   O("Build custom servers for both so that every tool description is written in the team's own style and wording.", False,
     "Consistent style does not justify maintaining a custom copy of a standard integration."),
   O("Use community servers for both and encode the refund approval rules as a long section in the system prompt.", False,
     "Approval rules are business logic that needs enforcement in your own tool or hook, not in a prompt."),
   O("Skip MCP and have the agent call each system through Bash with curl commands that developers write by hand.", False,
     "This gives up structured tool interfaces and increases shell-command risk.")],
  "Existing community MCP servers are preferable for standard integrations such as Jira or ticketing, while custom servers are reserved for team-specific workflows.",
  "Choosing community versus custom MCP servers (Task 2.4).",
  "One integration is generic and one is company-specific, so the answer splits along that line.",
  "Build what differentiates you. Reuse what doesn't.", diff="I")

Q("P2-Q10", "4.1",
  "Support leads want the agent to label each billing dispute with a priority from 1 to 3. Today the same dispute gets different priorities on different runs, and the label definitions in the prompt are 'high priority means important and urgent'. Which improvement is most effective?",
  [O("Define each priority with explicit criteria and a concrete example case (for example amount thresholds, account standing, and time sensitivity) so classification is consistent.", True,
     "Correct. Explicit severity criteria with concrete examples produce consistent classification."),
   O("Ask the agent to think carefully about urgency and to be consistent with how it labeled earlier disputes.", False,
     "Comparing with earlier labels needs memory the classifier may not have, and the criteria stay vague."),
   O("Ask for a numeric confidence score alongside each priority and use it to resolve disagreements between runs.", False,
     "Confidence does not define what each priority means."),
   O("Run each dispute through the classifier five times and use the most common label.", False,
     "Voting hides an undefined standard and multiplies cost.")],
  "Explicit severity criteria with concrete examples for each level achieve consistent classification. Vague definitions like 'important and urgent' leave the boundary to chance.",
  "Explicit severity criteria (Task 4.1).",
  "Different labels for the same dispute show the criteria are underspecified.",
  "Define categories so two reviewers, human or model, would agree.")

Q("P2-Q11", "5.2",
  "A customer asks the agent to match a lower price they found at a competitor's store for an item bought last week. The company policy only describes adjustments for its own price drops within 14 days. What should the agent do?",
  [O("Escalate to a human, because the policy is silent on competitor price matching, and include the facts of the request in the handoff.", True,
     "Correct. A gap in policy is a legitimate escalation trigger."),
   O("Approve the match under the own-site price adjustment rule since it is the closest rule available.", False,
     "Stretching a rule beyond what it says creates an unauthorized exception."),
   O("Decline the request because it is not covered, and close the case so the customer can look for other options.", False,
     "Declining may be wrong if the business would allow an exception, and the decision is not the agent's to make."),
   O("Offer a goodwill credit for a modest amount to settle the conversation and avoid involving others.", False,
     "Inventing compensation outside policy is also an unauthorized decision.")],
  "Escalation triggers include policy exceptions or gaps, not just complex cases. When policy is ambiguous or silent on the specific request, escalate rather than guessing.",
  "Escalating on policy gaps (Task 5.2).",
  "The policy covers own-site adjustments only, so competitor matching is a gap.",
  "Agents should not invent policy. They should surface its gaps.")

Q("P2-Q12", "5.2",
  "A customer says, 'I'm Maria Garcia, I need my last order refunded.' get_customer returns three accounts for 'Maria Garcia' in different cities. What should the agent do next?",
  [O("Ask the customer for an additional identifier, such as the email address or postal code on the account, before proceeding.", True,
     "Correct. Multiple matches require clarification instead of a guess."),
   O("Select the account with the most recent order, since that is the most likely one the customer means.", False,
     "Heuristic selection can target the wrong person and expose or refund the wrong account."),
   O("Proceed with the first account returned and mention to the customer which one was used.", False,
     "Informing the customer afterwards does not prevent an incorrect action on the wrong account."),
   O("Escalate immediately to a human because a common name makes the case too ambiguous to handle.", False,
     "A quick clarifying question usually resolves it, and escalating wastes the opportunity to resolve in first contact.")],
  "When tool results return multiple matches, the agent should ask for additional identifiers rather than choosing by heuristics.",
  "Ambiguity resolution with multiple matches (Task 5.2).",
  "Acting on the wrong account has financial and privacy consequences, so a clarifying question is the cheapest safe step.",
  "Ask before acting when identity is ambiguous.")

Q("P2-Q13", "5.2",
  "A customer writes angrily that a package is three days late and demands that something be done, but does not ask for a person. The agent can see the order, confirm the delay, and offer a replacement or refund under policy. What is the best response?",
  [O("Acknowledge the frustration, offer the replacement or refund the policy allows, and escalate only if the customer then insists on a human.", True,
     "Correct. The issue is within the agent's capability, and escalation follows an actual request."),
   O("Escalate immediately because strong negative sentiment indicates the case is too sensitive for an automated agent.", False,
     "Sentiment is not a reliable proxy for case complexity, and escalating a resolvable issue lowers first-contact resolution."),
   O("Ignore the tone and respond only with the order status so the exchange stays factual.", False,
     "Ignoring frustration is poor service and may make the customer insist on a human."),
   O("Ask the customer to rate their frustration from 1 to 10 and escalate if the number is above 7.", False,
     "A self-rated scale adds friction and does not measure whether the agent can solve the problem.")],
  "The agent should acknowledge frustration while offering resolution when the issue is within its capability, escalating only if the customer reiterates a preference for a human. Sentiment and confidence scores are unreliable proxies.",
  "Escalation versus resolution under frustration (Task 5.2).",
  "No human was requested and the fix is within policy, which makes resolution the right first move.",
  "Distinguish a request for a person from an expression of emotion.")

Q("P2-Q14", "5.1",
  "A coordinator gathers results from three parallel investigations (shipping, billing, and account history) into one prompt for the final response. The final reply often omits a key billing finding that appears in the middle of the combined text. What is the best way to organize the aggregated input?",
  [O("Put a short summary of the key findings at the beginning and organize the detailed results under explicit section headers.", True,
     "Correct. This mitigates position effects in long inputs."),
   O("Append the three results in random order each run so that no investigation is systematically placed in the middle.", False,
     "Randomizing hides the pattern but does not fix the omissions."),
   O("Increase the amount of detail in every section so the important finding is repeated several times throughout.", False,
     "Padding makes the input longer and does not guarantee the model uses the key finding."),
   O("Ask the final agent to re-read the combined text ten times before writing the response.", False,
     "Repetition inside one turn does not change how attention is distributed.")],
  "Models tend to process the beginning and end of long inputs more reliably than the middle. Placing key findings first and using explicit section headers for details mitigates this lost-in-the-middle effect.",
  "Position-aware input ordering (Task 5.1).",
  "The omitted finding sat in the middle of a long combined input, the classic position problem.",
  "Structure long inputs as summary first, details labelled after.")

Q("P2-Q15", "5.1",
  "The billing and shipping investigators each return a long narrative with reasoning steps. The coordinator's context fills quickly and its final synthesis loses detail. The coordinator needs only amounts, dates, statuses, and a relevance rating. What change is best?",
  [O("Change the investigators to return structured data, such as key facts, citations, and relevance scores, instead of long reasoning narratives.", True,
     "Correct. Upstream agents should return compact structured results when downstream context is limited."),
   O("Increase the coordinator's context by moving to a larger-window model and keep the narratives unchanged.", False,
     "More room delays the problem and does not improve signal density or attention."),
   O("Have the coordinator truncate each narrative to its first 500 characters before reading it.", False,
     "Truncation may cut off the facts that matter, which are not necessarily at the start."),
   O("Add a summarizer subagent that rewrites every narrative into a paragraph before the coordinator sees it.", False,
     "That adds another lossy step when the investigators could produce structured output directly.")],
  "When downstream agents have limited context budgets, upstream agents should return structured data (key facts, citations, relevance scores) instead of verbose content and reasoning chains.",
  "Structured upstream outputs for limited context (Task 5.1).",
  "The coordinator needs only a few fields, so verbose narratives waste its budget.",
  "Design agent outputs for their consumer, not for the producer's convenience.", diff="C")
